.TH GXWI-SERVICES-MANAGER 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-services-manager \- start, stop and look after the services on this machine, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-services-manager
.br
.B gxwi-services-manager \-\-definition
.I SERVICE
.br
.B gxwi-services-manager \-\-new
.SH DESCRIPTION
Services Manager lists every service defined on this machine, what each is
doing, and when its next timer runs. Find a service narrows the list by name
or description. The service picked is shown in full beside it: its state and
why, its process, the account it runs as, how long it has run, what it has
reported, and each timer, in local time. Start, Stop, Restart, Reload and
Reset are under it, and on each row's right-click menu. A command does not
hold up the window: it is followed, and what came of it is said.
.PP
It asks peinit, on its control socket, as the person looking, and does
nothing with more authority than they have. A command is offered only where
they hold the right it needs and it would do something in the service's
state. As shipped, Administrators may do anything with a service, and
everyone signed in may see what it is doing. Services they may not see but
whose definitions they may read are listed as Not yours to see. If peinit
does not let them in at all, the window says so, lists what definitions it
can, and offers no command.
.PP
The state is asked again every two seconds; F5, or Refresh, asks at once.
The registry's \%Machine\eSystem\eServices key is watched, so a definition
changed elsewhere shows straight away.
.PP
Edit definition... opens the service's definition in a window of its own,
and New service... a new one: this program again, with the options below.
Every field is shown by group, checked as peinit checks it, and saved, with
Save or Ctrl+S, in one transaction of only what changed. Changing a
definition needs the right to set values on the service's key, defining one
the right to make keys under \%Machine\eSystem\eServices, and deleting one
DELETE on its key; as shipped, only Administrators have them. Without them,
the definition is shown with every field fixed, and why.
.PP
Who may control it... and Who may change its definition... open the
permissions editor on the service's ServiceSecurity and on its key; Default
permissions... on the one every service without its own takes. What the
person may not change is shown and not offered. Logs... opens what the
service has logged in Event Viewer.
.PP
Without a desktop to open on, it says so and ends: on a terminal, svctl does
what this does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-definition " SERVICE"
Open the definition of
.I SERVICE
alone, in a window of its own.
.TP
.B \-\-new
Open an empty definition, to define a new service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-services-manager.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Services Manager.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svctl (8),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
Controlling services, Defining a service, and Who can manage a service, at
learn.peios.org.
